        "id": "ch13_3_2-1",
        "text": "Best practices of training data with Python\nWe have already highlighted how important the data is when training a machine learning\nmodel. In this section, we will highlight a few best practices and recommendations when\npreparing and using data to train your ML model. These are as follows:\n\u2022I As we mentioned previously, collecting a large set of data is of key importance (a\nfew thousand data records or at least many hundreds). The bigger the size of the\ndata, the more accurate the ML model will be.\n\u2022I Clean and refine your data before starting any training. This means that there\nshould not be any missing fields or misleading fields in the data. Python libraries\nsuch as pandas are very handy for such tasks.\n\u2022I Using a dataset without compromising the privacy and security of data is important.\nYou need to make sure you are not using the data of some other organization\nwithout the appropriate approval.\n\u2022I GPUs work well with data-intensive applications. We encourage you to use GPUs to\ntrain your algorithms for faster results. Libraries such as XGBoost, TensorFlow, and\nKeras are well known for using GPUs for training purposes.\n\u2022I When dealing with a large set of data for training, it is important to utilize the\nsystem memory efficiently. We should be loading the data in memory in chunks,\nor utilizing distributed clusters to process the data. We encourage you to use the\ngenerator function as much as you can.\n\u2022I It is also a good practice to watch your memory usage during data-intensive tasks\n(for example, while training a model) and free up memory periodically by forcing\ngarbage collection to release unreferenced objects.\nNow that we've covered the available Python libraries and the best practices of using\nPython for machine learning, it is time to start working with real code examples.",
